---
import DocsLayout from "../../components/docs/DocsLayout.astro";
import Code from "../../components/docs/Code.astro";
import Tag from "../../components/docs/Tag.astro";

// Tool list and config shapes follow packages/coding.sh/src/mcp/tools.ts and
// src/adapters/_shared/mcp.ts (what `coding.sh init` writes for each agent).
const TOOLS: [string, string][] = [
  ["session_current", "The session of the agent calling the tool: its summary and latest timeline."],
  ["sessions_list", "Captured sessions, newest first, with turns, tool errors, tokens, cost, failure flags and derails. Filter with flag, e.g. looping."],
  ["session_get", "One session as a digest: per-turn index and a timeline of prompts, replies, tool calls and model calls."],
  ["search", "Full-text search over prompts, replies and tool input/output across all sessions."],
  ["failures", "What went wrong lately: flagged sessions, frequent tool errors, prompts where the user pushed back."],
  ["latency", "Model-call p50/p95 and time to first token, plus the slowest tools and tool calls."],
  ["usage", "Tokens and cost by day, agent, model or repo, with the most expensive sessions."],
  ["subagents", "The subagents a session spawned, as a tree with durations and errors."],
  ["experiments_list", "Dataset experiments with pass rate and confidence interval."],
  ["experiment_compare", "Paired comparison of two experiments: difference, p-value, regressed and improved cases."],
  ["harness_compare", "Harness A/B results from coding.sh compare: the list, or one report with the ship gate, item gate and own-tests split."],
  ["benchmarks", "Benchmarks: the list, one run's leaderboard with paired verdicts and worst failures, or one metric over time."],
  ["eval_from_session", "Draft an eval task from a session. The only tool that writes; agents ask you first."],
  ["status", "Health of coding.sh: version, available update, recent captures, pending spool, last error."],
];

const init = `coding.sh init          # hooks + MCP server for every detected agent
coding.sh init --no-mcp # hooks only
coding.sh mcp config    # print the server command for manual setup`;

const claude = `claude mcp add -s user coding-sh -- coding.sh mcp`;

const claudeJson = `// ~/.claude.json
{
  "mcpServers": {
    "coding-sh": { "type": "stdio", "command": "coding.sh", "args": ["mcp"], "env": {} }
  }
}`;

const codex = `# ~/.codex/config.toml
[mcp_servers.coding-sh]
command = "coding.sh"
args = ["mcp"]`;

const cursor = `// ~/.cursor/mcp.json
{
  "mcpServers": {
    "coding-sh": { "command": "coding.sh", "args": ["mcp"] }
  }
}`;

const opencode = `// ~/.config/opencode/opencode.json
{
  "mcp": {
    "coding-sh": { "type": "local", "command": ["coding.sh", "mcp"], "enabled": true }
  }
}`;

const gemini = `// ~/.gemini/settings.json
{
  "mcpServers": {
    "coding-sh": { "command": "coding.sh", "args": ["mcp"] }
  }
}`;
---

<DocsLayout>
  <p>
    <code>coding.sh mcp</code> is a local MCP server over stdio. It gives an agent read access to the sessions coding.sh captured on this
    machine, including the one it is running in, so it can answer "what failed last time?", "why was that slow?" or "what did this cost?"
    from real traces. Nothing leaves your machine.
  </p>

  <h2 id="automatic">You don't set it up <Tag status="built" /></h2>
  <p>
    <code>coding.sh init</code> shows one plan: capture hooks and the MCP server for every agent it finds. You answer once. It registers the
    server at user scope with Claude Code, Codex, Cursor, OpenCode and older Gemini CLI installs, backing up each file first. The install script runs
    <code>init</code> for you.
  </p>
  <Code code={init} />
  <p>
    The entry points at the installed CLI by absolute path, so agents started from a dock or an IDE find it without your shell's
    <code>PATH</code>. <code>coding.sh uninstall</code> removes it. A config file nobody touched since init gets its original bytes back.
    Otherwise only the <code>coding-sh</code> entry is removed.
  </p>
  <p>
    Every call reads fresh data. The server ingests new hook events before it answers, and the hook starts a background ingest when a
    session ends, so there is no <code>coding.sh ingest</code> step.
  </p>

  <h2 id="tools">Tools</h2>
  <div class="doc-table">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Tool</th><th>What it returns</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        {
          TOOLS.map(([name, desc]) => (
            <tr>
              <td>
                <code>{name}</code>
              </td>
              <td>{desc}</td>
            </tr>
          ))
        }
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    Resources: <code>codingsh://latest</code> and <code>codingsh://session/&lt;id&gt;</code>, each a Markdown digest of one session. When a
    newer coding.sh is out, the server's instructions mention it once and the <code>status</code> tool has the details.
  </p>

  <h2 id="manual">Manual config</h2>
  <p>
    For a machine where you'd rather not run <code>init</code>, or a project-scoped setup. These use the <code>coding.sh</code> binary on
    <code>PATH</code>; <code>coding.sh mcp config</code> prints the absolute-path form.
  </p>
  <h3 id="claude-code">Claude Code</h3>
  <Code code={claude} />
  <Code title="json" code={claudeJson} />
  <h3 id="codex">Codex</h3>
  <Code title="toml" code={codex} />
  <h3 id="cursor">Cursor</h3>
  <Code title="json" code={cursor} />
  <h3 id="opencode">OpenCode</h3>
  <Code title="json" code={opencode} />
  <h3 id="gemini">Gemini CLI (older installs)</h3>
  <Code title="json" code={gemini} />
</DocsLayout>
